{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794561534831,"sessionID":"ses_e2bccad09ffe2W451DarRh6OXK","part":{"id":"prt_1d433eb6f001K4FpslF1VjjF1A","sessionID":"ses_e2bccad09ffe2W451DarRh6OXK","messageID":"msg_1d433d35f0012gHzdvSndSE2kL","type":"step-start","snapshot":"a8d2d8fb96fb1932747d5023d811e511aeb04e17"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794561535192,"sessionID":"ses_e2bccad09ffe2W451DarRh6OXK","part":{"partID":"prt_1d433eb78002JS12RsvERFMRHA","sessionID":"ses_e2bccad09ffe2W451DarRh6OXK","messageID":"msg_1d433d35f0012gHzdvSndSE2kL","type":"tool","id":"call_8de20938405c4df6850c7650","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md","offset":161,"limit":80},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md, lines 161-240\n161: ### Wave 4, verifier follow-ups\n162: - [ ] T062 Replace the stderr case that passes without the cap (`SUBT`). The case `bounds stderr from a child that floods it and exits non-zero` passes with or without the cap in `SUB`, because `runAdvisorSubprocess` returns stderr through `sanitizeStderr`, which already cuts it to 240 characters. The one stderr reading that depends on the cap is the busy check: `runAdvisorSubprocess` matches `SQLITE_BUSY_PATTERN` against the collected stderr and retries once on a match, so a busy marker written after `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` characters is seen only when stderr is not capped. Replace that case with `it('stops reading stderr at the cap, so a busy marker past it is not seen', ...)`. Its script, written with the file's `writeScript`, waits for stdin to end as the other case does, writes `'e'.repeat(1024 * 1024)` and then `'\\ndatabase is locked\\n'` to stderr and sets `process.exitCode = 1`. Call `runAdvisorSubprocess` with the same options as the stdout case and expect `result.ok` to be `false`, `result.errorCode` to be `'NON_ZERO_EXIT'` and `result.retriesAttempted` to be `0`. Without the cap the marker is read, the call retries once and returns `SQLITE_BUSY_EXHAUSTED` with `retriesAttempted` 1. Keep the stdout case, keep the local `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` only if a case still reads it and change nothing in `SUB`. The cap is already in, so do not remove it to watch the case fail and do not touch git state: say in your report why the case would fail without the cap. Add a comment above the case saying the returned stderr is cut short whatever the cap, so the retry decision is the one reading that shows where collection stopped. Verify: `VX tests/subprocess-output-cap.vitest.ts` reports `2 passed (2)`, `rg -c \"database is locked\" $SUBT` prints 1 and `rg -c \"toBeLessThanOrEqual\" $SUBT` prints nothing.\n163: - [ ] T063 Restore the bench header and make only the one header change asked for (`BENCH`). The five comment lines under the module banner were rewritten into eight when only one line needed to change. Run `git -c core.fsmonitor=false show HEAD:.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/bench/hook-brief-signal-noise.bench.ts | sed -n 4,8p` and put those five lines back in place of the eight comment lines that now sit between the banner and the first `import`. Then change only the first of them, `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),`, to `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),`. Leave the other four restored lines as `HEAD` has them. Put the sentence about invented readings on its own comment line directly above the `pi:` row of `SEVEN_AXIS_MATRIX`, indented as the rows are: `// An invented axis reading would pass for a measured one, so a runtime nobody probed reads not-recorded.` Keep the five `not-recorded` rows and the realigned `claude` and `opencode` rows as they are. Verify: `VB` reports `4 passed (4)`, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg '^[-+]//'` prints exactly the two lines `-// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),` and `+// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),` and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg -c '^\\+\\s+// An invented axis reading'` prints 1.\n164: - [ ] T064 Count a hook as wired only when `node` stands where a shell starts a command (`HW`, `HWT`). Test first: in `HWT`, add three cases built with `writeClaudeSettings`, each with a `UserPromptSubmit` group holding one `{ type: 'command', command: <cmd> }` hook. With `node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` the check prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `/opt/homebrew/bin/node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `echo node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `hook-missing claude .claude/settings.json` and exits 1. Today the first passes, the second fails on `hook-missing` and the third fails on `ok claude`. Then rewrite `runsMarker`. A token, after `stripQuotes`, is a `node` executable when it equals `node` or ends with `/node`. It counts only at a command position: the first token of the command, a token right after `&&`, `||`, `;` or `|`, or the token right after `-c` when the token before `-c` is `bash` or `sh`. Report a match when such a token is followed by a token ending in the runtime's marker. These positions cover every real shape in this repository: `.cursor/hooks.json` starts its command with `node`, and `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json` run `bash -c 'cd \"...\" && node <marker> || ...'`. Add a comment above `runsMarker` saying an argument that only names `node`, as `echo node <script>` does, runs nothing, so `node` must stand where a shell starts a command. Verify: before the change `NT $HWT` reports `fail 2`, after it `NT $HWT` reports `fail 0`, and `node $HW --root .` prints `ok claude`, `ok codex`, `ok cursor` and `ok devin` and exits 0.\n165: - [ ] T065 Keep a failed restore inside the write-fault branch from escaping `main` (`EMB`, `EMBT`). `restoreFromBackup` never calls the write hook, because it restores with `fs.copyFileSync` and `fs.rmSync`, so the test makes its own read of the backup's `manifest.json` fail. Test first: in `EMBT`, add a case after `a write that throws mid-apply restores the backup and exits 3`, built like it on the `claude-only` fixture with `--create-router`. Take `backup` from the plan's output with `field(planned, 'backup')` and pass that output to `trackBackup(t, ...)`, as the damaged-backup case does. On its second call the write hook writes `not json` to `path.join(backup, 'manifest.json')` and then throws the same `EACCES` error the existing case throws. Expect `embed.main` to return 3 and the output to hold `ERROR: writing`, `ERROR: the restore failed: `, a line starting `backup:`, a line starting `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL`, and not to hold `restored:`. Today `main` throws the `SyntaxError` from `JSON.parse`. Then in the `catch` of `commandApply`, wrap the `restoreFromBackup` call in its own `try` and `catch`, and on a throw print `${TAG} ERROR: the restore failed: <message>` with `output.warn`. The `backup:`, `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL` lines and `return 3` follow on both paths as they do today. Add a comment saying the backup and undo lines are the operator's only way back once the restore itself fails, so a second fault must not swallow them. Verify: before the change `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 1` on the new case, after it `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0` and `node $PL/probe-embed-write-fault.cjs` still prints `main returned 3`.\n166: - [ ] T066 Move the exit 3 comment beside the code it explains (`EMB`). The four comment lines that start `// A write that throws partway through is neither a run that wrote nothing nor a finished` sit alone between `USAGE` and the `3. ERRORS` section banner. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `try {` that wraps the write loop in `commandApply`, below `let writing = null;` with one blank line between that line and the comment. Leave one blank line between `].join('\\n');` and the section banner where they were. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $EMB` exits 0, `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0`, `rg -n -A1 \"of its own rather than borrowing one the usage already spends on another meaning\" $EMB` prints the comment line and then a line ending `try {`, and that comment line's number is greater than the one `rg -n \"^function commandApply\" $EMB` prints.\n167: - [ ] T067 Say which status wins when the engine and the hook check disagree (`DSC`, `DSCT`). Test first: in `DSCT`, add one assertion to the case `the symlink output block maps the hook-wiring check exits to WARN and FAIL`: `assert.match(SYMLINK_CHECK.output, /When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK\\./)`. Then add the line `When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK.` as the last line of the `output: |` block in `DSC`, indented two spaces as the lines above it are. Verify: before the change `NT $DSCT` reports `fail 1`, after it `NT $DSCT` and `NT $DS/tests/doctor-symlink-contract.test.cjs` report `fail 0`.\n168: - [ ] T068 Run the CLI when it is launched through a symlink (`R`, `S`, `BG`). Both files compute `invokedDirectly` as `resolve(process.argv[1]) === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`. Node gives a linked entry file its real path in `import.meta.url` but leaves `process.argv[1]` as typed, so a launch through a link, for example under `.claude/skills/`, prints nothing and exits 0. Test first: in `BG`, add `describe('entry guard', ...)` with one case. It makes a temporary folder with `mkdtempSync`, links `<tmp>/runner.mjs` to `R` and `<tmp>/scorer.mjs` to `S` with `symlinkSync` and removes the folder in `finally`. Find each real path with `fileURLToPath(new URL('../scripts/routing-accuracy/<file name>', import.meta.url))`, and add `symlinkSync` to the `node:fs` import and `fileURLToPath` from `node:url`. Run each link with `spawnSync(process.execPath, [<link>, ...args], { encoding: 'utf8' })`. The runner link with `['--list', 'g2']` must exit 0 with the first stdout line `g2-deep-research-ask`. The scorer link with no arguments must exit 2 with stderr holding `--runtime is required with --locator`. Neither starts a runtime. Today both fail, because each link prints nothing and exits 0. Then in both files compute `invokedDirectly` by comparing `realpathSync` of `process.argv[1]` with `realpathSync` of `fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`, and treat a throw from `realpathSync`, as for an `argv[1]` that names no file, as not invoked directly. `R` already imports `realpathSync` and `S` must add it to its `node:fs` import. Add one sentence to each file's comment above `invokedDirectly` saying the entry path is compared after resolving links, because Node names a linked entry by its real path. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-behaviour-guards.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `node --check $R` and `node --check $S` exit 0 and `G2SUM` prints its planning line. Then set `L=\"$(mktemp -d)\"`, run `ln -s \"$PWD/$R\" \"$L/r.mjs\"` and `ln -s \"$PWD/$S\" \"$L/s.mjs\"`, ch... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n169: - [ ] T069 Move the arena marker comment beside the write (`ARB`). The three comment lines starting `// The runner refuses a live arm in a checkout without this file` sit above `const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';`. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `writeFileSync` call in `buildArena` that writes the marker under `join(dest, '.git', MARK)`. Leave one blank line between the `GIT_PREFIX` constant and the `MARK` constant. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $ARB` exits 0, `rg -c \"skilled-behaviour-arena\" $ARB` prints 1 and `rg -n -A1 \"its copied build trees\\.\" $ARB` prints the comment line and then the line holding `writeFileSync(join(dest, '.git', MARK)`.\n170: - [ ] T070 Read the Pi session header from the contained path (`$SR/pi.mjs`, `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, `$SR/*.d.mts`, `$SR/*.d.mts.map`). `diskResult` opens the session file for its header with `headerOf(filePath)` before `guardSessionPath` checks that the path stays inside the store, so a session file that links out of the store is opened before the refusal. Test first: in `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, add `test('a session file linked outside the store is refused without opening it', ...)` inside `withTempAsync('pi-outside', ...)`. Build `piSession({ root, projectDir })` after making `projectDir` as the other cases do. Write `join(root, 'outside-pi.jsonl')` with `writeJsonl`, holding the header `{ type: 'session', version: 3, id: SESSION, timestamp: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', cwd: projectDir }` and one user `message` row shaped like the fixture's `pi-u1` row. Replace `fixture.filePath` with a link to it, `rmSync(fixture.filePath)` and then `symlinkSync(outside, fixture.filePath)`. Start `trackFileOpens()`, call `readSession({ runtime: 'pi', sessionId: SESSION, ...fixture.options })` and call `tracker.restore()` in `finally`. Expect `ok` to be `false`, `code` to be `session_mismatch` and `tracker.opened` to hold neither `fixture.filePath` nor the outside path. Import `rmSync` and `symlinkSync` from `node:fs` and `trackFileOpens` and `writeJsonl` from `./helpers.mjs`. Today the code matches and the open check fails, because `headerOf` opens the link. Then in `diskResult`, right after `filePath` is built, call `resolveInside({ path: filePath, root })` and return its result when it is not `ok`. Read the header with `headerOf(contained.path)` and pass `contained.path` as `path` to `guardSessionPath`. Import `resolveInside` beside `guardSessionPath` from `./identity.mjs`. Add a comment saying the header is read from the path the containment check resolved, so a link out of the store is refused before any byte of it is read. Then run `SRD` and do not edit a declaration by hand. Verify: ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n171: - [ ] T071 Name the owner skill, not the raw row id, on a needs-a-mode head (`RD`, `GT`). A `needsMode` recommendation whose id has a generated command row, such as `command-create-agent`, renders `command-create-agent (needs a mode)`, because `commandLabel` returns `null` for it and the head falls back to the raw id. Test first: in `GT`, tighten the case `renders needs a mode even when the id has a command row` so it also expects the head to hold `use sk-doc (needs a mode)` and not to hold `command-`. Today it fails, because the head reads `use command-create-agent (needs a mode)`. Then in `renderAdvisorBrief`, change the `topLabel` fallback from `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? top.skill)` to `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? (top.needsMode === true ? commandRowFor(top.skill)?.ownerSkillId : undefined) ?? top.skill)`. Add a comment saying a recommendation that needs a mode is named by the skill that owns its row, because the row id is an internal key the reader cannot run. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `VX tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n172: - [ ] T072 Fail the holdout check on a corpus that marks no holdout row (`SLT`). Start only after T041 ends, because WG9 owns this file until then. In the case `publishes no holdout row`, add `expect(holdoutIds.size, 'the corpus marks no holdout row, so this check would pass without testing anything').toBeGreaterThan(0)` right after `holdoutIds` is built and before the key checks. Add a comment saying an empty set would make the check pass on a corpus that no longer marks its holdout rows. It passes today and fails the day the `split` field goes. Read only `id` and `split` from `CORPUS` and never print a holdout prompt. Verify: `VX tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"holdoutIds.size\" $SLT` prints 1.\n173: - [ ] T073 Tighten three README sentences the verifier found loose (`RA`). Start only after T055 ends, because WG10 owns this file until then. In the runner row, replace the sentence `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no other credential in the operator's shell reaches it.` with `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it.` In the capture row, change `the command attached to each row` to `the command attached to each published row`. In the `scorer-eval-baseline.json` row, change ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire and intent buckets`` to ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire, intent buckets and unknown count``, and add `` `capturedAtSha`,`` after `` `capturedAt`,`` in the same row. Change nothing else in the file. Verify: `rg -c \"the locale and XDG prefixes\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"no other credential\" $RA` prints nothing, `rg -c \"attached to each published row\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"capturedAtSha\" $RA` prints at least 1, `rg -c \"intent buckets and unknown count\" $RA` prints 1 and ``rg -c '^\\| `[^`]+\\.(mjs|py|jsonl|json)` \\|' $RA`` prints 21.\n174: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n175: \n176: ---\n177: \n178: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n179: ## Phase 3: Verification\n180: \n181: - [ ] T056 [C] Rebuild and run every suite (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, then `VALL`, `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 npx vitest run tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, `VB`, `DALL` and `VSR`, and write each command with its result lines into `after.txt`. Verify: the build exits 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with more passed tests than `$SP/before.txt`, the marker run reports 0 failed, `VB` reports `4 passed (4)` and `DALL` and `VSR` report `fail 0`.\n182: - [ ] T057 [C] Hold every floor (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `SRD --check`, `DERIVE`, `RV`, `SRM`, `G2SUM` and `HOLD`, write each beside its before line and end the file with `RESULT: every floor holds` only when it does. Verify: no `FLOOR` value and no `EVAL` value in its first three places is lower than its before line, the fourth `EVAL` value is no higher than 5, `TC`, `RV` and `SRM` exit 0, `SRD --check` prints `current`, `DERIVE` prints `fresh`, `G2SUM` equals its before line and `HOLD` prints `\"rows\":0`.\n183: - [ ] T058 [C] Rerun the reproductions (`$SP/repro-after.txt`). Run the six probes in `PL` after the build and append their output. Verify: the file holds `main returned 3`, `restored line: true`, `after: CLAUDE.md`, `plan exit=2`, a `ci.yml` line with equal digests, `undefined` on the no-edit, `/deep:ai-council` and both file-name lines of `probe-attach.mts`, `'COMMAND_POLICIES' in module -> false`, `(needs a mode)` on the first `probe-render.mjs` line and `(parent-named only)` on its second, and `probe-postwrite-foreign.cjs` prints 15 `apply exit 0` lines.\n184: - [ ] T059 [C] Build one arena and read its marker (`$SP/arena-smoke.txt`). Run `node $ARB --source . --dest <a fresh folder under mktemp -d>/arena`, then `test -f <arena>/.git/skilled-behaviour-arena` and `node -e \"import('./$R').then((m) => console.log(JSON.stringify(m.liveArmRefusal(process.argv[1]))))\" <arena>`, write the outputs to `arena-smoke.txt` and remove the temporary folder. Start no runtime in it. Verify: the builder prints `\"ok\":true`, the `test -f` exits 0, the refusal prints `null` and `test -e <the temporary folder>` exits 1 at the end.\n185: - [ ] T060 [C] Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md $ADV/lib/scorer/text.ts` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9]|WG[0-9]|DR[0-9]-P|R[0-9]-P[0-9]|iter[0-9]-P|Q[0-9]-)\"` prints nothing, the same `rg` over `CAG`, `BG`, `SUBT` and `DSCT` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- '*package.json' '*package-lock.json'` prints nothing, `rg -n \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**'` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n186: - [ ] T061 [C] Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence (`implementation-summary.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`). Run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs --folder <this folder> --apply` first. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n187: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n188: \n189: ---\n190: \n191: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n192: ## Completion Criteria\n193: \n194: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n195: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n196: - [ ] `$SP/after.txt` ends `RESULT: every floor holds` and `$SP/repro-after.txt` holds every fixed output T058 names\n197: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n202: ## Cross-References\n203: \n204: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n205: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n206: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n207: - **Review report**: `../review/review-report.md`\n208: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## Verification Checklist\n213: \n214: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n215: ## Verification Protocol\n216: \n217: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n218: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n219: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n220: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n221: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n222: \n223: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n224: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n225: \n226: ---\n227: \n228: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n229: ## Pre-Implementation\n230: \n231: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the reproductions are in `scratch/` (T001, T002), and `rg -c \"^order:\" scratch/before.txt` prints 1 (T003).\n232: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] Each task line ends with a verify clause: `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9].*Verify: ' tasks.md` and `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9]' tasks.md` print the same number.\n233: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n238: ## Code Quality\n239: \n240: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `TC` exits 0 and `node --check` passes on every changed `.mjs` and `.cjs` file.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 241]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md, lines 161-240\n161: ### Wave 4, verifier follow-ups\n162: - [ ] T062 Replace the stderr case that passes without the cap (`SUBT`). The case `bounds stderr from a child that floods it and exits non-zero` passes with or without the cap in `SUB`, because `runAdvisorSubprocess` returns stderr through `sanitizeStderr`, which already cuts it to 240 characters. The one stderr reading that depends on the cap is the busy check: `runAdvisorSubprocess` matches `SQLITE_BUSY_PATTERN` against the collected stderr and retries once on a match, so a busy marker written after `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` characters is seen only when stderr is not capped. Replace that case with `it('stops reading stderr at the cap, so a busy marker past it is not seen', ...)`. Its script, written with the file's `writeScript`, waits for stdin to end as the other case does, writes `'e'.repeat(1024 * 1024)` and then `'\\ndatabase is locked\\n'` to stderr and sets `process.exitCode = 1`. Call `runAdvisorSubprocess` with the same options as the stdout case and expect `result.ok` to be `false`, `result.errorCode` to be `'NON_ZERO_EXIT'` and `result.retriesAttempted` to be `0`. Without the cap the marker is read, the call retries once and returns `SQLITE_BUSY_EXHAUSTED` with `retriesAttempted` 1. Keep the stdout case, keep the local `MAX_OUTPUT_CHARS` only if a case still reads it and change nothing in `SUB`. The cap is already in, so do not remove it to watch the case fail and do not touch git state: say in your report why the case would fail without the cap. Add a comment above the case saying the returned stderr is cut short whatever the cap, so the retry decision is the one reading that shows where collection stopped. Verify: `VX tests/subprocess-output-cap.vitest.ts` reports `2 passed (2)`, `rg -c \"database is locked\" $SUBT` prints 1 and `rg -c \"toBeLessThanOrEqual\" $SUBT` prints nothing.\n163: - [ ] T063 Restore the bench header and make only the one header change asked for (`BENCH`). The five comment lines under the module banner were rewritten into eight when only one line needed to change. Run `git -c core.fsmonitor=false show HEAD:.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/bench/hook-brief-signal-noise.bench.ts | sed -n 4,8p` and put those five lines back in place of the eight comment lines that now sit between the banner and the first `import`. Then change only the first of them, `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),`, to `// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),`. Leave the other four restored lines as `HEAD` has them. Put the sentence about invented readings on its own comment line directly above the `pi:` row of `SEVEN_AXIS_MATRIX`, indented as the rows are: `// An invented axis reading would pass for a measured one, so a runtime nobody probed reads not-recorded.` Keep the five `not-recorded` rows and the realigned `claude` and `opencode` rows as they are. Verify: `VB` reports `4 passed (4)`, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg '^[-+]//'` prints exactly the two lines `-// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (claude/opencode),` and `+// Invokes the brief renderer per runtime (the iteration measured Claude and OpenCode, and the rest read not-recorded),` and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- $BENCH | rg -c '^\\+\\s+// An invented axis reading'` prints 1.\n164: - [ ] T064 Count a hook as wired only when `node` stands where a shell starts a command (`HW`, `HWT`). Test first: in `HWT`, add three cases built with `writeClaudeSettings`, each with a `UserPromptSubmit` group holding one `{ type: 'command', command: <cmd> }` hook. With `node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` the check prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `/opt/homebrew/bin/node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `ok claude` and exits 0. With `echo node ${CLAUDE_MARKER}` it prints `hook-missing claude .claude/settings.json` and exits 1. Today the first passes, the second fails on `hook-missing` and the third fails on `ok claude`. Then rewrite `runsMarker`. A token, after `stripQuotes`, is a `node` executable when it equals `node` or ends with `/node`. It counts only at a command position: the first token of the command, a token right after `&&`, `||`, `;` or `|`, or the token right after `-c` when the token before `-c` is `bash` or `sh`. Report a match when such a token is followed by a token ending in the runtime's marker. These positions cover every real shape in this repository: `.cursor/hooks.json` starts its command with `node`, and `.claude/settings.json`, `.codex/hooks.json` and `.devin/hooks.v1.json` run `bash -c 'cd \"...\" && node <marker> || ...'`. Add a comment above `runsMarker` saying an argument that only names `node`, as `echo node <script>` does, runs nothing, so `node` must stand where a shell starts a command. Verify: before the change `NT $HWT` reports `fail 2`, after it `NT $HWT` reports `fail 0`, and `node $HW --root .` prints `ok claude`, `ok codex`, `ok cursor` and `ok devin` and exits 0.\n165: - [ ] T065 Keep a failed restore inside the write-fault branch from escaping `main` (`EMB`, `EMBT`). `restoreFromBackup` never calls the write hook, because it restores with `fs.copyFileSync` and `fs.rmSync`, so the test makes its own read of the backup's `manifest.json` fail. Test first: in `EMBT`, add a case after `a write that throws mid-apply restores the backup and exits 3`, built like it on the `claude-only` fixture with `--create-router`. Take `backup` from the plan's output with `field(planned, 'backup')` and pass that output to `trackBackup(t, ...)`, as the damaged-backup case does. On its second call the write hook writes `not json` to `path.join(backup, 'manifest.json')` and then throws the same `EACCES` error the existing case throws. Expect `embed.main` to return 3 and the output to hold `ERROR: writing`, `ERROR: the restore failed: `, a line starting `backup:`, a line starting `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL`, and not to hold `restored:`. Today `main` throws the `SyntaxError` from `JSON.parse`. Then in the `catch` of `commandApply`, wrap the `restoreFromBackup` call in its own `try` and `catch`, and on a throw print `${TAG} ERROR: the restore failed: <message>` with `output.warn`. The `backup:`, `undo:` and `STATUS=FAIL` lines and `return 3` follow on both paths as they do today. Add a comment saying the backup and undo lines are the operator's only way back once the restore itself fails, so a second fault must not swallow them. Verify: before the change `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 1` on the new case, after it `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0` and `node $PL/probe-embed-write-fault.cjs` still prints `main returned 3`.\n166: - [ ] T066 Move the exit 3 comment beside the code it explains (`EMB`). The four comment lines that start `// A write that throws partway through is neither a run that wrote nothing nor a finished` sit alone between `USAGE` and the `3. ERRORS` section banner. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `try {` that wraps the write loop in `commandApply`, below `let writing = null;` with one blank line between that line and the comment. Leave one blank line between `].join('\\n');` and the section banner where they were. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $EMB` exits 0, `NT $EMBT` reports `fail 0`, `rg -n -A1 \"of its own rather than borrowing one the usage already spends on another meaning\" $EMB` prints the comment line and then a line ending `try {`, and that comment line's number is greater than the one `rg -n \"^function commandApply\" $EMB` prints.\n167: - [ ] T067 Say which status wins when the engine and the hook check disagree (`DSC`, `DSCT`). Test first: in `DSCT`, add one assertion to the case `the symlink output block maps the hook-wiring check exits to WARN and FAIL`: `assert.match(SYMLINK_CHECK.output, /When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK\\./)`. Then add the line `When the engine and the hook-wiring check end on different statuses, the worse one wins: FAIL over WARN over OK.` as the last line of the `output: |` block in `DSC`, indented two spaces as the lines above it are. Verify: before the change `NT $DSCT` reports `fail 1`, after it `NT $DSCT` and `NT $DS/tests/doctor-symlink-contract.test.cjs` report `fail 0`.\n168: - [ ] T068 Run the CLI when it is launched through a symlink (`R`, `S`, `BG`). Both files compute `invokedDirectly` as `resolve(process.argv[1]) === fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`. Node gives a linked entry file its real path in `import.meta.url` but leaves `process.argv[1]` as typed, so a launch through a link, for example under `.claude/skills/`, prints nothing and exits 0. Test first: in `BG`, add `describe('entry guard', ...)` with one case. It makes a temporary folder with `mkdtempSync`, links `<tmp>/runner.mjs` to `R` and `<tmp>/scorer.mjs` to `S` with `symlinkSync` and removes the folder in `finally`. Find each real path with `fileURLToPath(new URL('../scripts/routing-accuracy/<file name>', import.meta.url))`, and add `symlinkSync` to the `node:fs` import and `fileURLToPath` from `node:url`. Run each link with `spawnSync(process.execPath, [<link>, ...args], { encoding: 'utf8' })`. The runner link with `['--list', 'g2']` must exit 0 with the first stdout line `g2-deep-research-ask`. The scorer link with no arguments must exit 2 with stderr holding `--runtime is required with --locator`. Neither starts a runtime. Today both fail, because each link prints nothing and exits 0. Then in both files compute `invokedDirectly` by comparing `realpathSync` of `process.argv[1]` with `realpathSync` of `fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)`, and treat a throw from `realpathSync`, as for an `argv[1]` that names no file, as not invoked directly. `R` already imports `realpathSync` and `S` must add it to its `node:fs` import. Add one sentence to each file's comment above `invokedDirectly` saying the entry path is compared after resolving links, because Node names a linked entry by its real path. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-behaviour-guards.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `node --check $R` and `node --check $S` exit 0 and `G2SUM` prints its planning line. Then set `L=\"$(mktemp -d)\"`, run `ln -s \"$PWD/$R\" \"$L/r.mjs\"` and `ln -s \"$PWD/$S\" \"$L/s.mjs\"`, ch... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n169: - [ ] T069 Move the arena marker comment beside the write (`ARB`). The three comment lines starting `// The runner refuses a live arm in a checkout without this file` sit above `const MARK = 'skilled-behaviour-arena';`. Move them, wording unchanged and indented two spaces, to sit directly above the `writeFileSync` call in `buildArena` that writes the marker under `join(dest, '.git', MARK)`. Leave one blank line between the `GIT_PREFIX` constant and the `MARK` constant. Change no code. Verify: `node --check $ARB` exits 0, `rg -c \"skilled-behaviour-arena\" $ARB` prints 1 and `rg -n -A1 \"its copied build trees\\.\" $ARB` prints the comment line and then the line holding `writeFileSync(join(dest, '.git', MARK)`.\n170: - [ ] T070 Read the Pi session header from the contained path (`$SR/pi.mjs`, `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, `$SR/*.d.mts`, `$SR/*.d.mts.map`). `diskResult` opens the session file for its header with `headerOf(filePath)` before `guardSessionPath` checks that the path stays inside the store, so a session file that links out of the store is opened before the refusal. Test first: in `$SRT/pi.test.mjs`, add `test('a session file linked outside the store is refused without opening it', ...)` inside `withTempAsync('pi-outside', ...)`. Build `piSession({ root, projectDir })` after making `projectDir` as the other cases do. Write `join(root, 'outside-pi.jsonl')` with `writeJsonl`, holding the header `{ type: 'session', version: 3, id: SESSION, timestamp: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', cwd: projectDir }` and one user `message` row shaped like the fixture's `pi-u1` row. Replace `fixture.filePath` with a link to it, `rmSync(fixture.filePath)` and then `symlinkSync(outside, fixture.filePath)`. Start `trackFileOpens()`, call `readSession({ runtime: 'pi', sessionId: SESSION, ...fixture.options })` and call `tracker.restore()` in `finally`. Expect `ok` to be `false`, `code` to be `session_mismatch` and `tracker.opened` to hold neither `fixture.filePath` nor the outside path. Import `rmSync` and `symlinkSync` from `node:fs` and `trackFileOpens` and `writeJsonl` from `./helpers.mjs`. Today the code matches and the open check fails, because `headerOf` opens the link. Then in `diskResult`, right after `filePath` is built, call `resolveInside({ path: filePath, root })` and return its result when it is not `ok`. Read the header with `headerOf(contained.path)` and pass `contained.path` as `path` to `guardSessionPath`. Import `resolveInside` beside `guardSessionPath` from `./identity.mjs`. Add a comment saying the header is read from the path the containment check resolved, so a link out of the store is refused before any byte of it is read. Then run `SRD` and do not edit a declaration by hand. Verify: ... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n171: - [ ] T071 Name the owner skill, not the raw row id, on a needs-a-mode head (`RD`, `GT`). A `needsMode` recommendation whose id has a generated command row, such as `command-create-agent`, renders `command-create-agent (needs a mode)`, because `commandLabel` returns `null` for it and the head falls back to the raw id. Test first: in `GT`, tighten the case `renders needs a mode even when the id has a command row` so it also expects the head to hold `use sk-doc (needs a mode)` and not to hold `command-`. Today it fails, because the head reads `use command-create-agent (needs a mode)`. Then in `renderAdvisorBrief`, change the `topLabel` fallback from `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? top.skill)` to `sanitizeSkillLabel(metadataSkillLabel(result) ?? (top.needsMode === true ? commandRowFor(top.skill)?.ownerSkillId : undefined) ?? top.skill)`. Add a comment saying a recommendation that needs a mode is named by the skill that owns its row, because the row id is an internal key the reader cannot run. Verify: before the change `VX tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts` reports 1 failed, after it 0 failed, `VX tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `TC` exits 0.\n172: - [ ] T072 Fail the holdout check on a corpus that marks no holdout row (`SLT`). Start only after T041 ends, because WG9 owns this file until then. In the case `publishes no holdout row`, add `expect(holdoutIds.size, 'the corpus marks no holdout row, so this check would pass without testing anything').toBeGreaterThan(0)` right after `holdoutIds` is built and before the key checks. Add a comment saying an empty set would make the check pass on a corpus that no longer marks its holdout rows. It passes today and fails the day the `split` field goes. Read only `id` and `split` from `CORPUS` and never print a holdout prompt. Verify: `VX tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` reports 0 failed and `rg -c \"holdoutIds.size\" $SLT` prints 1.\n173: - [ ] T073 Tighten three README sentences the verifier found loose (`RA`). Start only after T055 ends, because WG10 owns this file until then. In the runner row, replace the sentence `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics and each runtime's own prefixes plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no other credential in the operator's shell reaches it.` with `A live turn gets only the allowlisted environment, the shell basics, the locale and XDG prefixes, each runtime's own prefixes and the Skilled switch prefixes, plus the GitHub and git overrides, so no GitHub token or git credential helper from the operator's shell reaches it.` In the capture row, change `the command attached to each row` to `the command attached to each published row`. In the `scorer-eval-baseline.json` row, change ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire and intent buckets`` to ``the fixture hashes and `metrics` for the full corpus, holdout, ambiguity slice, gold-none false fire, intent buckets and unknown count``, and add `` `capturedAtSha`,`` after `` `capturedAt`,`` in the same row. Change nothing else in the file. Verify: `rg -c \"the locale and XDG prefixes\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"no other credential\" $RA` prints nothing, `rg -c \"attached to each published row\" $RA` prints 1, `rg -c \"capturedAtSha\" $RA` prints at least 1, `rg -c \"intent buckets and unknown count\" $RA` prints 1 and ``rg -c '^\\| `[^`]+\\.(mjs|py|jsonl|json)` \\|' $RA`` prints 21.\n174: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-2 -->\n175: \n176: ---\n177: \n178: <!-- ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n179: ## Phase 3: Verification\n180: \n181: - [ ] T056 [C] Rebuild and run every suite (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `npm run build` in `ADV`, then `VALL`, `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1 npx vitest run tests/command-brief-golden.vitest.ts tests/hooks/skill-advisor-cli-fallback-envelope.vitest.ts tests/render-child-session.vitest.ts` in `ADV`, `VB`, `DALL` and `VSR`, and write each command with its result lines into `after.txt`. Verify: the build exits 0, `VALL` reports 0 failed with more passed tests than `$SP/before.txt`, the marker run reports 0 failed, `VB` reports `4 passed (4)` and `DALL` and `VSR` report `fail 0`.\n182: - [ ] T057 [C] Hold every floor (`$SP/after.txt`). Run `FLOOR`, `EVAL`, `TC`, `SRD --check`, `DERIVE`, `RV`, `SRM`, `G2SUM` and `HOLD`, write each beside its before line and end the file with `RESULT: every floor holds` only when it does. Verify: no `FLOOR` value and no `EVAL` value in its first three places is lower than its before line, the fourth `EVAL` value is no higher than 5, `TC`, `RV` and `SRM` exit 0, `SRD --check` prints `current`, `DERIVE` prints `fresh`, `G2SUM` equals its before line and `HOLD` prints `\"rows\":0`.\n183: - [ ] T058 [C] Rerun the reproductions (`$SP/repro-after.txt`). Run the six probes in `PL` after the build and append their output. Verify: the file holds `main returned 3`, `restored line: true`, `after: CLAUDE.md`, `plan exit=2`, a `ci.yml` line with equal digests, `undefined` on the no-edit, `/deep:ai-council` and both file-name lines of `probe-attach.mts`, `'COMMAND_POLICIES' in module -> false`, `(needs a mode)` on the first `probe-render.mjs` line and `(parent-named only)` on its second, and `probe-postwrite-foreign.cjs` prints 15 `apply exit 0` lines.\n184: - [ ] T059 [C] Build one arena and read its marker (`$SP/arena-smoke.txt`). Run `node $ARB --source . --dest <a fresh folder under mktemp -d>/arena`, then `test -f <arena>/.git/skilled-behaviour-arena` and `node -e \"import('./$R').then((m) => console.log(JSON.stringify(m.liveArmRefusal(process.argv[1]))))\" <arena>`, write the outputs to `arena-smoke.txt` and remove the temporary folder. Start no runtime in it. Verify: the builder prints `\"ok\":true`, the `test -f` exits 0, the refusal prints `null` and `test -e <the temporary folder>` exits 1 at the end.\n185: - [ ] T060 [C] Check the boundaries. Verify: `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --quiet HEAD -- AGENTS.md specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/spec.md $ADV/lib/scorer/text.ts` exits 0, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff HEAD -U0 -- .skilled | rg \"^\\+.*(//|#|\\*).*(specs/|REQ-0|ADR-0|T0[0-9]{2}|phase [0-9]|WG[0-9]|DR[0-9]-P|R[0-9]-P[0-9]|iter[0-9]-P|Q[0-9]-)\"` prints nothing, the same `rg` over `CAG`, `BG`, `SUBT` and `DSCT` prints nothing, `git -c core.fsmonitor=false diff --stat HEAD -- '*package.json' '*package-lock.json'` prints nothing, `rg -n \"COMMAND_POLICIES\" .skilled --glob '!**/node_modules/**'` prints nothing and `git -c core.fsmonitor=false status --short -- .skilled` lists only files named in this phase's tasks.\n186: - [ ] T061 [C] Write `implementation-summary.md` and mark each row in `acceptance-criteria.md` with its evidence (`implementation-summary.md`, `acceptance-criteria.md`). Run `node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/repair-derived.cjs --folder <this folder> --apply` first. Verify: `bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <this folder> --strict` prints `RESULT: PASSED`.\n187: <!-- /ANCHOR:phase-3 -->\n188: \n189: ---\n190: \n191: <!-- ANCHOR:completion -->\n192: ## Completion Criteria\n193: \n194: - [ ] All tasks marked `[x]`\n195: - [ ] No `[B]` blocked tasks remaining\n196: - [ ] `$SP/after.txt` ends `RESULT: every floor holds` and `$SP/repro-after.txt` holds every fixed output T058 names\n197: <!-- /ANCHOR:completion -->\n198: \n199: ---\n200: \n201: <!-- ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n202: ## Cross-References\n203: \n204: - **Specification**: See `spec.md`\n205: - **Plan**: See `plan.md`\n206: - **Acceptance criteria**: See `acceptance-criteria.md`\n207: - **Review report**: `../review/review-report.md`\n208: <!-- /ANCHOR:cross-refs -->\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## Verification Checklist\n213: \n214: <!-- ANCHOR:protocol -->\n215: ## Verification Protocol\n216: \n217: | Priority | Handling | Completion Impact |\n218: |----------|----------|-------------------|\n219: | **[P0]** | HARD BLOCKER | Cannot claim done until complete |\n220: | **[P1]** | Required | Must complete OR get user approval |\n221: | **[P2]** | Optional | Can defer with documented reason |\n222: \n223: A blocked task gets `[B]` and one line naming what blocks it. A moved floor blocks the phase.\n224: <!-- /ANCHOR:protocol -->\n225: \n226: ---\n227: \n228: <!-- ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n229: ## Pre-Implementation\n230: \n231: - [ ] CHK-001 [P0] The before numbers and the reproductions are in `scratch/` (T001, T002), and `rg -c \"^order:\" scratch/before.txt` prints 1 (T003).\n232: - [ ] CHK-002 [P0] Each task line ends with a verify clause: `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9].*Verify: ' tasks.md` and `rg -c '^- \\[[ xB]\\] T[0-9]' tasks.md` print the same number.\n233: <!-- /ANCHOR:pre-impl -->\n234: \n235: ---\n236: \n237: <!-- ANCHOR:code-quality -->\n238: ## Code Quality\n239: \n240: - [ ] CHK-010 [P0] `TC` exits 0 and `node --check` passes on every changed `.mjs` and `.cjs` file.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 241]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/specs/system-skill-advisor/routing-and-scoring/008-command-surfacing-build/014-review-remediation/tasks.md\",\"offset\":161,\"limit\":80}"},"time":{"start":1794561534840,"end":1794561535192}}}}
